WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.610
So this is HIV.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.300
It's a typical retrovirus,

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:09.050
meaning that it has an outer envelope. And in the center it has two copies of RNA,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.880
as well as an enzyme, here in blue,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.380
that's reverse transcriptase,

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:17.390
which will ultimately turn that RNA into DNA.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.080
The virus itself,

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:24.590
with this outer envelope protein actually directly 

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.720
infects T helper cells.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.190
The way that it does this, is that as it comes up to the cell's surface,

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:36.990
it uses receptors that are on T helper cells, and exclusive to T helper 

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:37.440
cells,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.090
which are CD4 for molecule,

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.220
which really defines T helper cells,

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:47.130
it's a surface receptor that binds to the envelope protein. That 

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.000
causes a confirmational change and allows a second receptor to grab hold of the 

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:52.570
envelope.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.050
This is the Chemokine coreceptor,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.060
it's also called CCR5,

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.850
and we'll talk about that more.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.450
What happens now is that the stock of the envelope protein 

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.760
pierces through from the virus into the host 

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.740
cell and starts to draw the two cell memberanes-- the cell membrane in the viral membrane 

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:13.440
together.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.360
And what ultimately happens is fusion of those two membranes and the viral genetic 

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.150
material is injected essentially into the cell,

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.930
and the envelope protein is left at the cell surface.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:32.250
The virus has a matrix and a capsid protein, shown here in green and red, that 

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.140
essentially are digested when it enters into the cell. That releases 

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:42.000
viral enzymes in the viral RNA. And here we have reverse 

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:42.850
transcriptase,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.760
which takes the viral RNA, and using host 

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.130
nucleotides,

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:52.350
converts that viral RNA into a single strand of DNA.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.870
While it does that it makes some random errors,

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.240
which is characteristic of reverse transcriptase,

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.055
it has very poor proofreading activity. That single-stranded 

00:02:03.575 --> 00:02:07.995
DNA now is again reverse transcribed into a double-stranded 

00:02:08.415 --> 00:02:09.105
DNA.

00:02:11.355 --> 00:02:12.515
At that point,

00:02:12.525 --> 00:02:15.895
another enzyme that has come in with the virus in the beginning,

00:02:15.895 --> 00:02:17.005
called integrase,

00:02:17.355 --> 00:02:22.125
essentially grabs hold of that double stranded DNA, and carries it through a 

00:02:22.125 --> 00:02:25.105
nuclear pore into the nucleus of the cell.

00:02:26.835 --> 00:02:28.455
Within the nucleus of the cell,

00:02:28.455 --> 00:02:33.095
it finds the host chromosome and it basically the integrase enzyme 

00:02:33.215 --> 00:02:37.845
makes a nick in the host DNA and allows for HIV to 

00:02:37.845 --> 00:02:40.435
insert itself into the host chromosome.

00:02:40.755 --> 00:02:45.105
And that right there, is what establishes lifelong infection. 

00:02:46.545 --> 00:02:50.625
Now, RNA polymerase comes along and makes mRNA. Those 

00:02:53.625 --> 00:02:57.335
mRNAs encode for different viral proteins.

00:02:57.555 --> 00:03:02.335
They end up associating with ribosomes at the surface of the rough endoplasmic 

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:03.385
reticulum.

00:03:03.915 --> 00:03:07.395
And here's a piece of mRNA that's making envelope protein,

00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:11.325
which is directly produced into the endoplasmic reticulum,

00:03:11.845 --> 00:03:14.815
and it's shuttled then through the endoplasmic reticulum,

00:03:15.245 --> 00:03:17.295
and taken to the cell's surface,

00:03:17.425 --> 00:03:17.985
where,

00:03:17.985 --> 00:03:22.755
at the cell's surface, it becomes embedded in the cellular membrane, and at this 

00:03:22.755 --> 00:03:26.495
point, coalescing with other envelope proteins that have been produced,

00:03:26.735 --> 00:03:31.695
you have this cluster of envelope proteins now on the surface of this infected 

00:03:31.695 --> 00:03:32.205
cell.

00:03:33.485 --> 00:03:34.805
At the same time,

00:03:35.535 --> 00:03:40.485
there are other mRNAs that are being produced that allow for 

00:03:40.495 --> 00:03:43.125
translation of other viral proteins.

00:03:43.345 --> 00:03:46.445
So here are additional viral proteins being made,

00:03:46.445 --> 00:03:51.315
which we're going to be used to make up the key components that the 

00:03:51.315 --> 00:03:53.205
virus ultimately is going to need.

00:03:54.095 --> 00:03:59.035
These are transported again to the cell surface to the area where these envelope proteins 

00:03:59.035 --> 00:04:03.942
are, and a strand of RNA, as well as some of the enzymes, 

00:04:03.952 --> 00:04:05.792
are part of that complex.

00:04:07.732 --> 00:04:10.842
This then buds off at the cell surface at this point,

00:04:10.842 --> 00:04:12.552
but it's still not a mature virion,

00:04:12.782 --> 00:04:17.452
because the polyprotein chain needs to still be 

00:04:17.462 --> 00:04:20.052
digested into its component parts.

00:04:20.362 --> 00:04:25.252
That's done by an enzyme called protease. Protease breaks up those 

00:04:25.262 --> 00:04:29.962
polyprotein chains, and ultimately allows for them to coalesce and 

00:04:29.962 --> 00:04:34.612
form the mature structures that make up the final 

00:04:34.612 --> 00:04:34.972
virion.

00:04:35.452 --> 00:04:36.952
And now you have a mature,

00:04:36.952 --> 00:04:37.452
infectious,

00:04:37.452 --> 00:04:41.162
virion that can go on now to infect other cells.

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:44.612
Once that happens now,

00:04:44.622 --> 00:04:47.162
the cell can produce tons of viruses,

00:04:47.352 --> 00:04:51.112
and this is really what then keeps the whole process going.

